@flexiarg inbox-zero/classify-the-dirt
@title Classify The Dirt By How Much Judgement It Needs
@keywords classification, unpushed, generated-noise, real-work, dead-worktree, judgement
@audience inbox-zero gate, sweepers, promotion planners
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status draft 2026-09-16 (claude-7; from futon0/README-inbox-zero.md "Two kinds of dirt" and "Why fifty sweeps did not hold"; not yet implemented as a classifier — check-clean lists failing clauses without classifying)
@references [inbox-zero/measure-against-the-remote inbox-zero/push-the-declared inbox-zero/ignore-by-kind inbox-zero/promote-at-turn-end]
@see-also buffer-cleaner/classify-staleness
@why fifty sweeps did not hold because every sweep treated one undifferentiated pile, and a pile containing judgement cannot be automated. On 2026-08-19 the fleet's 2,610 dirty files and 35 unpushed commits were four problems with opposite remedies: 35 commits needing no judgement, ~2,012 files of one missing ignore pattern needing judgement once per kind, ~68 files of real work needing a person per file, and dead worktrees needing a retirement path.
@how classify each failing item into exactly one class with its remedy: committed-but-unpushed (push; no judgement) · generated noise, untracked (describe the kind in .gitignore; once per kind) · generated output, tracked (regenerate-and-commit or untrack; once per kind) · real uncommitted work (a person or turn-end promotion; per item) · dead or ephemeral worktree (retire through the owning lease path, never raw removal) · unclassifiable (typed, reported, never folded into another class). Record the class and the evidence for it on every item.
@violation-signature a sweep that commits, ignores and discards from one list; a generated file committed as work or real work ignored as noise; an unclassified remainder silently dropped; a class decided by path enumeration rather than by kind.
! conclusion: Split failing items by the judgement their remedy needs, so the mechanical classes can act without a person and only real work waits for one.

  + context: The inbox-zero gate reports failing clauses per repo; the items under a clause are heterogeneous.

  + IF:
    A repo fails clause 1, 3 or 5 and something must decide what to do about each item.

  + HOWEVER:
    Treating the items as one pile forces the whole pile to the slowest remedy (a person), which is why it recurs; and tracked generated outputs (issue boards, rendered PDFs) fit neither "noise" nor "work".

  + THEN:
    Assign every item one class with recorded evidence, route each class to its own remedy, and keep an explicit unclassifiable class.

  + BECAUSE:
    The problem is not large once split: one timer, a handful of one-time pattern fixes, and a queue of about sixty files.

  + evidence: fleet census 2026-08-19 (35 commits / 2,012 noise / ~68 work); futon1b `*-store*/` fix 49cc714 removed 1,006 files per Linode in one rule; the 2026-09-16 21:01 gate run shows p4ng clause 1 consisting of nine tracked generated outputs (issue-board, sec-*-generated.tex, wm-status.pdf), a class the README's three did not name.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Implement the classifier as a pure function over the gate's per-clause report, with the tracked-generated class explicit.
